## Rule System
// IMPORTANT: The .cursor/rules directory is the source of truth for all implementation standards
// Each rule file defines specific requirements and tracks implementation status

### Rule File Structure
1. Status Tracking Format:
   - [DONE] - Feature fully implemented and tested
   - [IN_PROGRESS] - Currently being implemented
   - [PENDING] - Not yet started
   - [BLOCKED] - Waiting on dependencies

2. Rule-Test Connection:
   - Each rule maps to specific test files
   - Tests must be minimal and focused
   - Tests should not affect other system components
   - Implementation follows rule requirements exactly

3. Cross-Reference Format:
   ```
   // Implementation of:
   // - @rule-file.mdc#Section-Name
   
   // Referenced by:
   // - @other-rule.mdc#Section-Name
   ```

### Rule Implementation Strategy
1. Core Principles:
   - Start with rule documentation
   - Write minimal, focused tests
   - Implement core functionality
   - Track status rigorously

2. Test Implementation:
   - Tests mirror rule structure
   - Each test maps to specific rule section
   - Keep tests simple and focused
   - Avoid unnecessary complexity

3. Status Updates:
   - Update status in rule files
   - Track dependencies between rules
   - Note cross-rule impacts
   - Document completion criteria

## Testing Strategy
// Implementation of @testing-standards.mdc#Core-Testing-Principles

1. Test Organization:
   - Tests mirror source structure
   - Each test maps to specific rules
   - Maintain clear rule references
   - Track test coverage per rule

2. Test Implementation:
   - Write minimal, focused tests
   - Test only what's needed
   - Avoid unnecessary complexity
   - Maintain rule compliance

3. Test Execution:
   ```bash
   cd mobile
   npm test           # Run all tests
   npm run test:watch # Watch mode
   ```

## Development Guidelines
1. Code Organization:
   - Feature-first organization
   - Co-locate tests with source
   - Maintain clear dependencies
   - Follow React Native best practices

2. Design Principles:
   - Prioritize user experience
   - Maintain performance targets
   - Follow cyberpunk aesthetic
   - Support educational goals

3. Anti-Patterns to Avoid:
   - Over-engineering solutions before MVP
   - Implementing edge cases too early
   - Complex test infrastructure before core functionality
   - Handling all possible scenarios before basics work

4. MVP Development Focus:
   - Start with minimal viable implementation
   - Add complexity only when needed
   - Validate core functionality first
   - Expand test coverage gradually
   - Defer edge case handling
   - Keep infrastructure simple until required

5. Color Scheme:
   - Primary: #00ff00 (Matrix green)
   - Secondary: #ff00ff (Neon purple)
   - Background: #1a1a1a (Dark gray)
   - Accent: #00ffff (Cyan)
   - Warning: #ff0000 (Red)

